# Flow-manager browser-turn monitoring

This non-always-loaded carrier retains only the two manager-review canon sections
that `create-issue-draft` selects literally. Portable Browser-GPT manager mechanics
are owned by the runbook sections for
[start-of-shift preflight](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#start-of-shift-preflight),
[turn preparation](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#prepare-one-turn),
[launch](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#launch),
[observation and settlement](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#observe-and-settle),
[publication and cleanup](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#publication-and-tab-lifecycle),
[incident handling](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#incident-handling),
[one-shot diagnosis](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#one-shot-diagnosis), and
[handoff/close](../../docs/browser-gpt-turn-runbook.md#shift-handoffclose).
Long-running child mechanics remain owned by
[`docs/flow-manager-long-running-child-runbook.md`](../../docs/flow-manager-long-running-child-runbook.md).

## Launch and observation

For tracked create-issue and pack-review turns, use
`npm run chatgpt-browser-turn -- turn ...`. Applicable long-running manager
turns use `npm run flow-manager-browser-gpt-long-run -- ...` and its handoff
receipt. Before every tracked turn, the caller/orchestrator mints and retains one
non-empty `--invocation-id` and passes that exact value through the ordinary
transport or long-running adapter and every later harvest/finalization action
for the same attempt. Transport and adapter layers validate and forward this
identity; they must not mint, replace, or reinterpret it. Presence of
`--invocation-id` is common to ordinary and direct-publication-capable flows and
never selects direct mode by itself; direct mode requires its direct-only
arguments.

For governed create-Issue `direct-publication/v1` reviewer turns, the reviewer
input is not hand-written. `.cursor/skills/create-issue-draft/SKILL.md` owns the
ordered manager-review canon declaration and `scripts/lib/manager-review-brief.ts`
renders the exact **unmarked** transport input. Both ordinary and long-running
launches carry the bound `--stage` and `--source-slot` in addition to the
existing repository, Issue, source-revision, reviewer-source, and invocation
identity. The long-running adapter rejects missing direct-publication context
before child spawn.

Immediately before any profile verification, CDP connection, tab creation, or
send, the canonical state-light entry reads the stable input and independently
regenerates the expected reviewer bytes from the current tracked canon plus the
bound invocation context. Exact byte equality is admission authority. Caller-
carried source identities, hashes, manifests, or previously generated bytes are
not proof of origin. Missing/malformed context, unreadable/ambiguous canon, or a
byte mismatch settles through the existing `turn-result/v1` as
`state: input_invalid`, `scope: invocation`, `send_count: 0`, with a concrete
`canonical_prompt_*` cause and non-zero exit. A mismatch cause carries only the
expected/observed unmarked prompt SHA-256 values plus the ordered current
`path@blobSha` diagnostics; it never contains prompt bytes.

Before launch, use a trusted current checkout, the configured local headed
automation browser, and the applicable workflow/role. One invocation opens one
dedicated owned tab, sends one exact user payload, and observes that same tab.
Page/DOM completion is sufficient once the final assistant reply is stable and
no longer generating; PID, shell state, silence, log growth, service-terminal
witnesses, and heartbeats are not completion authority.
In a fresh worktree, copy `local.config.json` from the operator checkout into
the same worktree path, or set both required values before launch:
`export DISCUSS_WITH_GPT_PROJECT_URL="<PROJECT_URL>"` and
`export DISCUSS_WITH_GPT_CHROME_USER_DATA_DIR="<CHROME_USER_DATA_DIR>"`.
The `discuss-with-gpt: operator configuration missing` signature means the
mandatory configuration is not resolved; it can also occur with a copied but
incomplete config. Verify both `projectUrl` and `chromeUserDataDir`, or both
environment-variable equivalents.

The canonical prompt comparison covers only the unmarked reviewer input. After
successful admission, the transport-owned `OPKTURNV1...` marker is inserted per
payload exactly as before. Exactly one current user node with that marker proves
ownership and page-path delivery; zero or multiple matches fail closed. Product
ids and prompt-text matching are diagnostic only. Do not fabricate the marker in
a prompt or use a second monitor. No caller-authored bytes may be inserted
between canonical admission and the existing marker-wrap/send path. The helper
emits `turn-result/v1`; the long-running adapter additionally owns the terminal
envelope and handoff receipt. A direct event is invocation-local, must be
retained and reported, and must not gate siblings or the account.

## Legacy state and diagnostic probe

The legacy `scripts/chatgpt-browser-turn.ts` prompt-bearing `turn` is not a
second governed direct-publication sender. If that legacy CLI receives
`--reviewer-source-output` or any governed direct-publication identity/context
key, it refuses before browser configuration, profile verification, CDP, page,
or send with `input_invalid:legacy_direct_publication_turn_refused`. Existing
non-direct legacy diagnostics/control compatibility remains unchanged; the
canonical state-light path is the sole governed direct-publication sender.

`status/list`, `clear`, capability/Gate-B, `possible_delivery`, profile walls,
stale recovery/orphan records, locks, queues, leases, and shell liveness are
not admission or completion authority. Unexpected events are appended
best-effort to the local recurrence journal and included in the current report;
do not use that journal to authorize work.

When direct diagnosis is genuinely needed, run the sanctioned one-shot page
probe with local variables such as `${CDP_ENDPOINT}` and an exact target
identity. `list`, `inspect`, `export`, and `liveness` are diagnostic-only and
always carry `workflow_authority: none`; they never publish a recovered turn or
progress a workflow. `harvest` is the sole action-producing probe
(`diagnostic_only: false`) and also carries `workflow_authority: none`; it may
recover/publish only the exact owned turn for the supplied invocation identity.
The explicit `inspect --url ... --open-if-missing true` acquisition path may
create one owned page, observe it until readiness, and close exactly that owned
page before returning. Do not replace these paths with raw CDP, Playwright,
salvage scripts, selectors, JavaScript, or a watch loop.

For contract-evidence authoring, `binding-id` uses
`producer:datum:expected`, for example
`orchestrator-pack:$.status:closed`. The producer must be listed in the closed
`scripts/contract-evidence-producer-registry.json`; repo-owned examples include
`orchestrator-pack` and `orchestrator-pack-scripts`. This is authoring
guidance, not validator documentation.
